.TH gate 7 (date) "Linux man-pages (unreleased)"
.SH NAME
gate \- overview of the gate user-space synchronization mechanism
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <linux/gate.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Linux kernel provides the gate mechanism
as a building block
for simple, potentially fast user-space synchronization
between threads
belonging to the same process (thread group).
.P
It consists of two system calls,
.BR gate_wait (2)
and
.BR gate_wake (2),
which allow a thread
to atomically check a condition in memory and,
if the condition holds,
wait efficiently until woken by another thread.
.P
Gates offer a restricted interface compared to
.BR futex (2),
omitting features like priority inheritance
and complex requeueing operations.
This simpler design may offer performance advantages
in certain scenarios.
However,
gates are strictly limited to synchronization
.I within
a single thread group (process),
unlike futexes
which can synchronize unrelated processes using shared memory.
.P
The core idea is similar to the "slow path" concept used with futexes.
Applications typically attempt fast user-space atomic operations
(like compare-and-exchange) first.
Only when contention is detected
or a specific condition needs to be awaited
does the application invoke
.BR gate_wait (2)
to interact with the kernel and block the thread.
Another thread,
after changing the relevant state,
uses
.BR gate_wake (2)
to wake the specific waiting thread.
.P
A gate operates on a user-space memory location specified by
.IR uaddr .
The
.BR gate_wait (2)
call checks if the (potentially masked) value at
.I uaddr
matches an expected value;
if so,
it blocks.
The
.BR gate_wake (2)
call attempts to wake a specific thread (identified by
.IR pid )
if that thread is currently blocked in
.BR gate_wait (2)
on the exact same
.IR uaddr .
.P
Most programmers will likely use gates indirectly
through higher-level synchronization constructs
provided by threading libraries
or custom synchronization primitives built upon this mechanism.
Direct use requires careful understanding
of memory ordering and race conditions (see NOTES and CAVEATS).
.P
For the precise operational details,
argument descriptions,
return values,
and error codes of the system calls,
see
.BR gate_wait (2).
.SH VERSIONS
The gate mechanism and associated system calls
first appeared in Linux 6.14.
.SH NOTES
.SS Memory ordering
The kernel guarantees specific memory ordering semantics
for the
.BR gate_wait (2)
and
.BR gate_wake (2)
system calls,
which are crucial for correct synchronization between threads.
These guarantees act like full memory barriers
(conceptually similar to
.BR smp_mb ()
in the kernel),
ensuring memory operations are observed in the correct order
across CPUs.
.TP
.BR gate_wait (2):
Performs a full memory barrier
between registering an intent to wait in the kernel
and atomically reading the value at
.I uaddr
to check the wait condition.
However,
no specific memory barrier is implied by the kernel
.I after
the read
or
.I after
the thread wakes up (whether normally or spuriously).
.TP
.BR gate_wake (2):
Performs a full memory barrier
before attempting to unregister the wait intent
of the target thread
.I pid
and potentially waking that thread if it is sleeping.
However,
no specific memory barrier is implied by the kernel
.I after
it checks the target's state
or
.I after
it initiates the wakeup.
.P
This ensures that
when concurrent
.BR gate_wait (2)
and
.BR gate_wake (2)
calls are executing,
the call to
.BR gate_wait (2)
will either see the updated value at
.I uaddr
or it will see the notification from
.BR gate_wake (2).
.SS Write-then-wake pattern
Typically,
a thread calls
.BR gate_wake (2)
only
.B after
performing the state change
(usually a memory write,
often to
.I uaddr
itself or related data)
that the waiting thread is interested in.
For example,
writing an "unlocked" state to a lock variable
before calling
.BR gate_wake (2)
on a thread waiting to acquire it.
Without a preceding state change,
waking a thread may simply cause it
to re-check the (still unmet) condition
and immediately wait again via
.BR gate_wait (2).
.SH CAVEATS
.SS Typical usage: Atomic operation followed by wait loop
A primary use case for
.BR gate_wait (2)
is in the "slow path"
of user-space synchronization algorithms
after a fast-path atomic operation fails.
For example,
trying to acquire a lock using an atomic compare-and-exchange operation:
.IP (1) 5
Atomically attempt to change the state.
.IP (2)
If the atomic operation succeeds,
the critical section is entered or the update is done;
proceed.
.IP (3)
If the atomic operation fails,
then use
.BR gate_wait (2)
to wait until the value
.I might
change.
.IP (4)
Upon return from
.BR gate_wait (2),
regardless of it being a successful result
or an error result
.RB ( EAGAIN ,
.BR ETIMEDOUT ,
.BR EINTR ,
etc.),
the application must typically loop back to step (1).
Looping back to retry the atomic operation
is generally necessary because:
.RS 5
.IP \[bu] 3
The memory state at
.I uaddr
could have changed
between the kernel's action (wakeup, check, timeout)
and the return to user space
due to race conditions.
.IP \[bu]
Wakeups (a return value of 0) can be spurious.
.IP \[bu]
The timeout expiration may be racing
with the memory state at
.I uaddr
changing.
.RE
.P
Consequently,
retrying the primary atomic operation
is often the only reliable way
to determine if the desired state transition can now proceed.
.P
One way to do this for the above example is as follows:
.P
.in +4n
.EX
enum mtx_state { UNLOCKED, LOCKED };
void mtx_lock(enum mtx_state* lock)
{
    long           err = 0;
    enum mtx_state expected = UNLOCKED;
\&
    while (true) {
        /* Step (1). */
        if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(lock, &expected, LOCKED)) {
            /* Step (2). */
            return;
        }
\&
        /* Step (3). */
        syscall(__NR_gate_wait, lock, (__kernel_ulong_t) -1,
                (__kernel_ulong_t) expected, sizeof(*lock),
                (const struct gate_wake_options *) NULL,
                (size_t) 0);
        /* Step (4). */
    }
}
.EE
.in
.P
This loop structure,
centered around retrying the primary atomic operation after waiting,
is necessary to correctly handle race conditions,
spurious wakeups,
and state changes
that might occur concurrently when using
.BR gate_wait (2).
.SS Spurious Wakeups and Address Reuse Hazards
Applications must be prepared for
.BR gate_wait (2)
to return 0 unexpectedly.
This can happen in two main ways:
.IP (1) 5
.BR "Spurious Wakeups" :
The kernel may wake the thread spuriously,
without a corresponding
.BR gate_wake (2)
call,
signal,
or timeout event.
This is a common behavior permitted
for many blocking primitives.
.IP (2)
.BR "Address Reuse Races" :
The actions typically performed by a waking thread
before calling
.BR gate_wake (2)
(like writing to memory)
are not atomic with the
.BR gate_wake (2)
call itself.
This time window allows other threads to potentially run,
deallocate the memory at
.IR uaddr ,
and reallocate that same address
for a completely different purpose.
If this happens,
a subsequent
.BR gate_wake (2)
targeting the original waiter's
.I pid
might still wake that thread,
because the wake mechanism primarily checks
if the passed address
.I uaddr
matches the one stored when the wait began,
without necessarily validating the current semantic context
of that memory.
From the original thread's perspective,
this is an unexpected wakeup caused by the race condition.
.P
Because wakeups can be spurious
or arise from race conditions involving address reuse,
the application must always re-validate
the specific condition it is waiting for
after
.BR gate_wait (2)
returns 0,
typically by retrying its primary atomic operation
or re-reading relevant state
within its synchronization loop (see "Typical usage" above).
.SS Interpreting gate_wake() errors in concurrent contexts
In complex concurrent programs,
certain errors returned by
.BR gate_wake (2)
might result from intricate race conditions
rather than straightforward programming errors
in the calling thread:
.TP
.B EAGAIN
This indicates the target thread was found waiting,
but on a different address than
.IR uaddr .
While this could be due to the caller
specifying the wrong address,
it might also occur if the target thread was previously waiting on
.IR uaddr ,
was woken up (perhaps by an action related to
the current caller's preceding writes),
made progress,
and subsequently blocked again on a
.I different
address
before the
.BR gate_wake (2)
call was processed.
.TP
.B EFAULT
While potentially indicating the caller passed an invalid
.IR uaddr ,
this error can also arise from race conditions
involving memory deallocation.
If
.I uaddr
points into dynamically managed memory
(for example, a reference-counted object),
actions preceding the
.BR gate_wake (2)
call
might allow another thread to free and unmap this memory
before
.BR gate_wake (2)
executes.
In such cases,
.B EFAULT
might be a benign consequence of the memory lifecycle race,
not necessarily a bug in the caller's pointer handling.
.TP
.B ESRCH
This means no thread with the specified
.I pid
was found.
While this can indicate the caller used an invalid or stale
.IR pid ,
it could also occur due to a race
where the target thread existed,
was woken (perhaps by the caller's related actions),
completed its work,
and
.B exited
before the
.BR gate_wake (2)
system call executed.
.TP
.B EPERM
This indicates the caller and target thread
.I pid
belong to different thread groups.
This check normally prevents waking threads in other processes.
However,
it could also arise from a race condition involving PID reuse:
if the intended target thread (in the caller's thread group) exits,
its PID might be reused by the kernel
for a new thread in a
.I different
thread group.
If
.BR gate_wake (2)
executes after this PID reuse,
it will correctly find a thread with the numeric
.I pid
but return
.B EPERM
because the (new) target thread is no longer
in the caller's thread group.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR futex (2),
.BR futex (7),
.BR gate_wait (2),
.BR pthreads (7),
.BR syscall (2)
